---
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Recipe template. Copy it, rename it, fill it in, delete these comments.
#
#   cp _recipes/_TEMPLATE.md.example _recipes/dal-tadka.md
#
# The filename becomes the URL: dal-tadka.md → /recipes/dal-tadka/.
#
# Start here rather than from an existing recipe. Copying one works, but it also
# means the four derived fields arrive pre-filled with another dish's numbers —
# and a wrong number is believed exactly because it is present, where an absent
# one is not. Every field marked ⚠ below is one of those.
#
# There is no markup anywhere in this file, and there should be none in yours.
# A recipe is data; the layout builds both the page and the JSON-LD from it in
# one pass. If you need something the layout cannot express, extend the layout
# so every recipe gains it.
#
# Run `bundle exec rake` when you are done. It takes about four seconds and
# names anything missing or malformed.
#
# The full reference is README.md → "Recipe front matter". This file is the
# short version, in the order you fill it in.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

title: 
# One or two sentences. This is what an importer and a search result show.
description: 

# The small line above the title, on the page only. Something like
# "Instant Pot · One pot · Vegan".
eyebrow: 

# ⚠ DERIVE THIS. The names this dish is genuinely known by — not near
# neighbours, not translations you found. It exists so a reader searching for
# the name they know finds the page.
alternate_names:
  - 
  - 

# ⚠ DERIVE THIS. What this dish is *not*: the similar dish it gets confused
# with, and how it differs. A vague answer is no answer. See masala-rice.md for
# one that does the job.
disambiguating_description: >-
  

date: YYYY-MM-DD
# The modification date. Add it when you change the method or the quantities,
# not when you fix a typo. Leave it out on a new recipe.
# updated: YYYY-MM-DD

# Defaults to both maintainers. Set it to a name, or a list of names, if this
# recipe is someone else's.
# author: 

# ── Classification ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

cuisine: 
category:            # Main course, Side dish, Dessert…
cooking_method:      # Pressure cooking, Sauté, Slow cooking…

# Plain text, and the one place allergens reach the structured data — schema.org
# has no allergen property at all, so "contains milk" and "may contain
# sulphites" belong here as well as in `allergens:` below.
keywords:
  - 
  - 

# ⚠ DERIVE THESE. Claim only what the ingredient list actually supports, and
# check the compound ingredients before claiming anything: commercial asafoetida
# is usually cut with wheat flour, and ghee, paneer, curd and butter each rule
# out VeganDiet. When a claim depends on which brand the cook buys, leave it off
# and explain in `notes`.
#
# `label` is what the page shows; `schema` must be one of the eleven members of
# schema.org's RestrictedDiet enumeration — anything else is silently
# meaningless to an importer:
#
#   VeganDiet  VegetarianDiet  GlutenFreeDiet  LowLactoseDiet  LowSaltDiet
#   LowFatDiet  LowCalorieDiet  DiabeticDiet  HinduDiet  HalalDiet  KosherDiet
#
# HinduDiet is claimed where a recipe is beef-free, which is a fact about what
# is in the pot. HalalDiet and KosherDiet are never claimed here: they depend on
# how ingredients were sourced and slaughtered, which a recipe cannot know.
diets:
  - label: 
    schema: 

# ── Allergens ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Declared against the fourteen of EU 1169/2011 Annex II. Ids only — the wording
# lives in _data/allergens.yml so no two recipes phrase the same allergen
# differently, and the layout derives the "free from" list by subtracting what
# you declare from the fourteen. That means an id you forget makes the recipe
# claim it is free of something it is not.
#
# The fourteen ids:
#   gluten  crustaceans  eggs  fish  peanuts  soybeans  milk
#   nuts  celery  mustard  sesame  sulphites  lupin  molluscs
#
# Delete whichever of the three keys you do not need.
allergens:
  present:
    - id: 
      # Say how much, and whether it can be left out or picked out.
      note: 
  may_contain:
    - id: 
      # Say what it comes through, and how a cook would check.
      note: 
  # An optional caveat about the recipe as a whole.
  note: 

# ── Image ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Resize and strip EXIF *before* committing: roughly 1600 px wide, under 500 KB,
# JPEG. Git keeps every version of a binary forever, so an unprocessed phone
# photo bloats the clone permanently and deleting it later does not undo that.
# Phone photos can also carry GPS coordinates, and this repository is public.
#
# The hero crops to 4:3 and index thumbnails to 1:1, so keep the food away from
# the extreme edges. A recipe with no photograph is still welcome.
image: /assets/images/recipes/
# What a screen reader announces. Describe what is in the picture.
image_alt: 
# Optional, page only: something the picture does not say on its own.
# image_caption: 

# ── Times, yield and cost ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two spellings of each time, because PT45M is unreadable and "45 min" is
# unparseable. Keep them in agreement, and keep
# prep_time + cook_time == total_time — some importers reject a recipe whose
# durations disagree, and `rake` will fail before they get the chance.
yield:               # 4 servings
prep_time:           # PT15M
cook_time:           # PT45M
total_time:          # PT60M
prep_time_display:   # 15 min
cook_time_display:   # 45 min
total_time_display:  # 1 hr

# ⚠ DERIVE THIS. What the ingredients cost for the whole pot, not per serving.
# Round up. Say in a comment which shops you priced it at — an estimate whose
# basis is unstated cannot be checked or updated.
estimated_cost:
  currency: EUR      # an ISO 4217 code
  value: ""

# ── Contents ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Everything that is not an ingredient. Rendered as the Equipment list.
tools:
  - 

# Grouped for the reader; the structured data gets the flattened list, so a
# group name can never end up inside an ingredient.
#
# One ingredient is one line carrying quantity, unit and item together. A line
# broken in two arrives at an importer as two ingredients. Weights, not cups:
# in a sealed pot the ratio of grain to water decides the result.
ingredients:
  - name: 
    items:
      - 
      - 

# Every step names its function, level and duration, so the reader is never
# guessing which button to press.
#
# `continues: true` matters more than it looks. An importer reads each step in
# isolation, so a step that just says "add the onion" arrives with no appliance
# attached and loses its Sauté indicator. With `continues: true` the layout
# prepends the session for you — which is why the `text` of such a step is
# written in lowercase, starting mid-sentence.
#
# Say where a step commonly goes wrong, at the step where it matters: a Burn
# warning from an unscraped base, spices scorching on a setting that is too
# high. That is the part a reader cannot work out for themselves.
steps:
  - name: 
    appliance:       # Sauté, Pressure Cook, Natural Release — omit if manual
    setting:         # High / Medium / Low
    duration:        # 30 sec, 8 min
    text: >-
      

  - name: 
    appliance: 
    setting: 
    duration: 
    continues: true
    # image: /assets/images/recipes/step-something.jpg
    text: >-
      

# ⚠ DERIVE THESE. Per serving: add up the ingredient weights and divide by the
# yield. All twelve keys are required — the layout emits every one, and a recipe
# missing one fails the checks. State the serving size as a weight.
#
# In `note`, say the figures are an estimate and name whatever dominates them.
# Almost always that is the salt, the added fat, or both.
nutrition:
  serving_size: 
  calories:                    # 520 kcal
  protein_content:             # 13 g
  fat_content:                 # 17 g
  saturated_fat_content:       # 2 g
  unsaturated_fat_content:     # 15 g
  trans_fat_content:           # 0 g
  cholesterol_content:         # 0 mg
  carbohydrate_content:        # 83 g
  sugar_content:               # 4 g
  fiber_content:               # 7 g
  sodium_content:              # 1180 mg
  note: >-
    

# Page only. The place for what did not fit anywhere else: how another cooker
# differs and by how much, what to do with leftovers, and why a diet claim you
# might have expected is absent.
notes: >-
  

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The closing --- below is load-bearing, and its absence is the single hardest
# bug to spot in this repository. Without it Jekyll treats the whole file as
# content, titles the page from the filename, and still renders an index card —
# so the page looks finished while having silently lost its description, dates,
# diets, allergens and every time.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
---
